.TH PRIMES 1
.SH NAME
primes \- generate primes, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B primes
[
.I start
[
.I finish
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Primes
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's own
.IR primes (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/primes.c ),
not a behavioral workalike reverse-engineered from a host tool - see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Primes
prints, one per line, every prime from
.I start
through
.I finish
inclusive. If
.I finish
is omitted, it prints indefinitely (until interrupted); if
.I start
is also omitted, it reads a single line from standard input as
.I start
instead (blank lines are skipped). A
.I start
of 0 is treated as 1.
.I Finish
below
.I start
exits cleanly with no output;
.I finish
above 2\u53\d (the largest integer a 64-bit float can represent
exactly, which is also the largest number this program will ever
consider) is an error.
.PP
Uses the same segmented wheel sieve as the original: numbers below 230
come from a pre-computed table; above that, 8000-number-wide segments
are sieved one at a time using wheel-mod-30 increments, so the process
can run indefinitely without needing more memory as the range grows.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/primes.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/primes.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR primestest (1),
.IR factor (1)
.SH BUGS
Every number here is Limbo's
.B real
(IEEE double), exactly matching the C original's use of
.B double
for the same reason: exact integer representation up to 2\u53\d, beyond
what a 32-bit integer could hold. This program was never rewritten to
use exact/arbitrary-precision integers and inherits the original's own
range limit as a result, not a limit specific to this port.
.PP
The pre-listed table's element count was miscounted by one on the
first translation attempt (49 instead of the real 50), silently
dropping 229 from any range whose
.I start
fell in the table's own range - found by a test straddling the
230 boundary against an independent primality check, not by
inspection; see
.IR primestest (1).
